# iCanHazMusic – notes for coding agents
A native macOS music player (SwiftUI + AppKit, Swift package, no third-party dependencies).
Target: macOS 15.4+, Swift tools 5.10 (Swift 5 language mode).

## Layout
| Path | What |
|---|---|
| `src/` | The app (single SwiftPM executable target `iCanHazMusic`). |
| `tests/` | Swift Testing suite (`swift test`), see [Tests](#tests) below. |
| `dev-docs/` | Background notes: `tag-parsing.md` = what the macOS tag APIs do and why the reader is built the way it is; `playback.md` = why `AVAudioEngine` and not `AVPlayer` (AVFoundation's FLAC seeking/padding bugs), how the engine works, API quirks; `lyrics.md` = research for lyrics (where embedded lyrics live, LRCLIB API notes and a coverage test; not implemented yet). |
| `build.sh` | Builds the `.app` bundle in `dist/` (see [Building and running](#building-and-running)). |
| `Info.plist`, `res/` | Bundle metadata and resources copied into the bundle by `build.sh`. |

### `src/`
Split into UI and logic (still one SwiftPM target, so no `public` needed). UI may depend on Core, never the other way round. One view per file.

- `App/` – `@main` entry point, `AppDelegate` (on quit it stops playback first, which fades out, so the sound doesn't pop; `PlaybackState.stopAndWait()`) and `DockMenu` (the Dock icon's right-click menu: current track and transport commands, only shown while playing/paused, like in the old version; built fresh on every open).
- `Core/` – Logic. **Foundation only (plus system frameworks without UI such as CryptoKit): never import SwiftUI/AppKit here.** Things that need AppKit (opening URLs, alerts) are handed in by the UI as closures.
  - `Integrations/LastFM/` – the last.fm integration (API docs: https://www.last.fm/api/authspec). Credentials are **not in the sources**: `build.sh` writes `ICHM_LASTFM_API_KEY`/`ICHM_LASTFM_API_SECRET` from `.env` into the bundle's `Info.plist` (`ICHMLastFMAPIKey`/`ICHMLastFMAPISecret`), XOR-ed with `LastFMCredentials.obfuscationKey` and base64-encoded (obfuscation only, so they can't be read at a glance; `build.sh` reads the key from that line of `LastFMAPI.swift`, keep its format), `LastFMCredentials.bundled` reads and decodes them; without them (plain `swift build`) the integration is unavailable (Connect is disabled). Never put them into sources or tests (tests use dummy ones).
    - `LastFMAPI.swift` – credentials, `LastFMCall`, signing, request building, answer checking and the **error classification** (`LastFMError.Kind`: `authentication` = codes 4/9/10/17/26 -> disconnect; `temporary` = 8/11/16/29, 5xx, network, garbage -> retry; `rejected` = anything else -> drop that call).
    - `LastFMClient.swift` – the client (over an `HTTPTransport`, 30 s hard limit).
    - `LastFMQueue.swift` – one call at a time, in order, up to 10 tries with 1/2/4/... s backoff; pending now playing updates behind the head are replaced by newer ones; in memory only.
    - `LastFMAuth.swift` – token -> open the confirmation URL -> `getSession` every 5 s and on `checkNow()` (the app becoming active); error 14 = not confirmed yet.
    - `LastFMService.swift` – `LastFMService.shared`: connection state (config `integrations.lastfm.session`/`username`), connect/disconnect, and the `PlaybackListener` that enqueues now playing (track start) and scrobbles (track left after >= 50% listened, track >= 30 s). `onConnectionLost` is called after Last.fm refused the session (the service has disconnected itself by then; the UI alerts and bounces the Dock icon).
  - `Integrations/HTTPTransport.swift` – what every network request goes through: `HTTPTransport` (`URLSessionTransport` = hard timeout; tests use stubs), `HTTPAnswer` (status, body, `Retry-After`) and `URLRequest(appURL:timeout:)`, which sets **`User-Agent: iCanHazMusic/{version}`** (`AppConstants.userAgent`). Build every request of the app with it.
  - `Integrations/LRCLIB/` – lyrics from https://lrclib.net (docs: `docs/lyrics.md`). Off by default (config `integrations.lrclib.enabled`).
    - `LRCLIBAPI.swift` – the request (`GET /api/search?track_name=&artist_name=`, one request per track; `/api/get` only tolerates 2 s of duration difference) and `match`: a record is the track if artist and title are **exactly** the same (case/blank space ignored, nothing else) and its duration is within 10% (`durationTolerance`; not checked if the track's is unknown), it has lyrics and isn't an instrumental; synced beats plain, then same album, then nearest duration. Errors: `temporary` (network, 5xx, 429 with `Retry-After`, garbage) / `rejected` (other 4xx).
    - `LRCLIBClient.swift` – one lookup -> `Lyrics?`. `LRCLIBQueue.swift` – like `LastFMQueue` (one at a time, 10 tries, 1/2/4/... s backoff, but at least `Retry-After`; `spacing` of 300 ms between lookups as LRCLIB asks); only the lookup in progress and the newest one are kept (skipping through tracks); reports `LRCLIBOutcome` (`found`/`notFound`/`failed`) for each.
  - `Notifications/` – system notifications (`UserNotifications`) about playback, for when the app isn't in front. `NotificationService.shared` is a `PlaybackListener` (`isEnabled` = Preferences > General > Playback Notifications, config `general.playback_notifications`, default on; off = nothing is sent and the permission isn't asked for): a track that starts -> title, artist as subtitle, `album · m:ss` as body and the cached cover (`CoverStore`, by `PlayedTrack.coverKey`) as attachment; `playlistDidEnd` -> "Playlist ended"; `queueDidEnd` -> "Queue ended". It hands messages to a `NotificationDelivery` (tests use a stub); `SystemNotificationDelivery` is the real one: **every message uses the same request identifier, so each replaces the previous notification and only the latest one is ever shown**; it asks for permission on the first message, skips everything for a non-`.app` executable (`UNUserNotificationCenter.current()` throws there) and has no delegate on purpose (no banner while the app is in front). Notifications need a signed bundle: `build.sh` signs dev builds with `ICHM_SIGNING_IDENTITY` too (ad-hoc only without it).
  - `Updates/` – self update from GitHub releases (ported from iCanHazRepose). Only **stable** releases (not `prerelease`) that have an **`ichm.zip`** asset count; the version is read from the release title (`iCHM 0.4.0`, else the tag), so keep releasing that way.
    - `UpdateAPI.swift` – `SemVer`, `GitHubRelease`, `UpdateInfo`, `UpdateError`, and `UpdateAPI` (the request to `api.github.com/repos/deseven/iCanHazMusic/releases`, reading the answer, `latest(in:)` = newest eligible release, newer than the running app or not).
    - `UpdateInstaller.swift` – download the zip next to the app -> check the archive's entry names -> `ditto` -> the one `.app` must pass the strict signature check **and satisfy the designated requirement of the running app** (same signing identity; an ad-hoc dev build only accepts itself) -> `install-update.sh` waits for our pid, swaps the bundles and `open`s the new app. Refuses a non-`.app` executable and an unwritable install directory. Doesn't quit: the UI does (`NSApp.terminate`, so playback fades out first).
    - `UpdateService.swift` – `UpdateService.shared`: `isEnabled` ("Check for Updates", Preferences > General, config `general.check_for_updates`, default on) = automatic check 24 h after `start()` (not at launch, so a user who restarts often isn't pestered) and then every 24 h, reported through `onUpdateFound`; `check(manual:)` (works with `isEnabled` off, ignores the skipped version, throws `UpdateError.busy` while another check runs), `skippedVersion` (config `general.skipped_update`, only automatic checks respect it), `install(_:)`.
  - `Lyrics/`
    - `Lyrics.swift` – `Lyrics` (source `embedded`/`lrclib`, `plain`, optional `lrc` + parsed `lines`, `lineIndex(at:)`), `LRC` (parser: several timestamps per line, `[offset:]`, enhanced `<..>` tags dropped, metadata lines skipped) and `LyricsKey` (SHA-256 hex of lower-cased, trimmed artist + title + album). Lyrics in a file's tag that are an LRC text (>= 2 timed lines) count as synced.
    - `LyricsStore.swift` – SQLite `.cache/lyrics.sqlite` (like `CoverStore`): key -> source, plain, synced. Embedded lyrics are written by `ImportSession` when tags are read (always replacing; a file without artist/title tags is skipped); LRCLIB's never replace what is there.
    - `LyricsService.swift` – `LyricsService.shared`, a `PlaybackListener`: `current`/`currentKey` = lyrics of the playing track (from the store at track start; else, with LRCLIB on, one lookup through the queue, the result shows up when it arrives; a "no" is remembered until quit, a failure isn't). `isEnabled` ("Lyrics Support", Preferences > General, config `general.lyrics_support`, default on; off = no `current`, `lyrics`/`hasLyrics` are empty so no lyrics buttons, no LRCLIB lookups; embedded lyrics are still stored by `ImportSession`), `isLRCLIBEnabled` (persisted; the user's choice, kept while lyrics support is off) and `isLRCLIBActive` (= both; what the lookup follows, the Preferences switch shows it and is disabled while support is off), `lyrics(artist:title:album:)`/`hasLyrics` for any track, `refreshCurrent()` after tags were re-read. **Tracks without tags (`PlayedTrack.hasTags`: artist "Unknown Artist" or title "Unknown Track") are never looked up** (and not sent to Last.fm either).
  - `Search/` – fuzzy search over all playlists (opened by Playlist > Search, ⌘F, or the global hotkey).
    - `SearchSettings.swift` – `SearchOptions` (plain value handed to a search: `playlists`, `albums`, `fuzzy`, `tracksOnly`) and `SearchSettings.shared` (Preferences > Search, config `search.*`: `byPlaylistName`, `byAlbumName`, `fuzzy`, `preferAddingToQueue`; `options`). Playlists off = no playlist result; albums off = no album result **and** the album name (and a compilation's "Various Artists") isn't searched for tracks either, only their own artist + title; fuzzy off = `FuzzyMatcher.best(fuzzy: false)`, exact only. `PlaylistSearcher(store:settings:)` reads the options when a search starts.
    - `SearchText.swift` – `SearchText` (folds case/accents/width into UTF-16 arrays, splits the query into words) and `FuzzyMatcher`: a word is found *exactly* (a part of a field; better as the whole field / at its start / at a word start / in a short field) or else *fuzzy* (letters in order within a stretch of ~1.5x the word, or a word of the field with a typo: edit distance 1, 2 for words of 8+; words of <3 letters only exact, <4 no typos). Every word of the query has to be found in one of the fields; one fuzzy word makes the match fuzzy.
    - `PlaylistSearch.swift` – `SearchResult` (`playlist`/`album`/`track`; tracks and albums are referred to by `TrackID`, an album by its first track), `SearchIndex` (folded text of one `Playlist` instance), `SearchEngine.search` (pure; **playlists** are found by their whole name only (case/accents/extra spaces ignored, no fuzzy, no part of a name), only if there are several; **albums** by artist + title, **tracks** by artist + title + album; flat playlists have no albums; a track is dropped if its album is in the results and its own title doesn't match; order: found **playlists** first (always), then the playing playlist's entries (else the active one's), then the others'; in each group exact before fuzzy, then score, then album < track; at most `defaultLimit` = 10) and `PlaylistSearcher` (keeps an index per playlist instance in a task, rebuilt when a playlist's content is replaced; `prepare()` is called when the window opens).
  - `Config/`
    - `Config.swift`
    - `ConfigStore.swift` – `config.json`.
    - `ConfigLimits.swift` – validation limits.
  - `Playlists/`
    - `PlaylistStore.swift` – playlist list + **every playlist kept in memory** (search needs them all): the active one is read async first (`isLoading`; it is what the UI gets as `activePlaylist`), then the others one by one in the background (`isPreloading`, `startPreload`); one selected before its turn is read at once; nothing is unloaded except on delete (`loaded`, `playlist(named:)`, `loadedPlaylists`, `lastPlayed(in:)`), switching to a loaded playlist is instant. Changes go through `commit` (memory + the UI's `activePlaylist`) and are saved in the background. It also does the edits that replace the active content: `remove(ids:)`, `applyTags` (reloaded tags), `setFlat`, plus `updateTrack(id:…)` (duration/format pushed from playback), all telling `PlaybackState.playlistWasReplaced` so the playing track is found again by its ID. It keeps each loaded playlist's `last_played` (`setLastPlayed`, called by playback whenever a track starts; `lastPlayedRow`; saves are coalesced) outside the immutable `Playlist`, and counts `openCount` (a playlist became active with its content in place), which the UI reacts to by selecting and scrolling to the last played row.
    - `PlaylistFile.swift` – file format.
    - `TrackEntry.swift` – one flat stored track, and `TrackID`.
      - **Track IDs**: every track has a positive number unique within its playlist, stable through regrouping, removals, tag reloads and playback updates, never reused (`next_id`). The same file added twice = two IDs. `Playlist` hands them out itself (an import's tracks come in with `unassignedTrackID`; missing/duplicate IDs in a file are repaired on load) and looks tracks up by them (`position(of:)`, `row(of:)`, `track(id:)`). Refer to tracks by ID wherever a reference has to outlive a playlist edit; `(album, track)` positions (`TrackPosition`) and row numbers are only valid for one `Playlist` instance.
    - `Playlist.swift` – the flattened album/track row model. `movingTrack(id:toIndexInAlbum:)` / `movingAlbum(_:to:)` reorder (drag and drop; `nil` = nothing changes); `PlaylistStore.moveTrack`/`moveAlbum` apply them **synchronously** (cheap, and the UI needs the answer at once), store and tell playback like `replaceActive` does (`install`). Stored order = album order by first appearance + track order inside an album, which `AlbumBuilder` keeps on load.
    - `PlaylistExchange.swift` – `PlaylistFormat` (`m3u8`/`m3u`/`pls`) and reading/writing those files. Export writes absolute paths, UTF-8, with titles/durations. Import takes **only the paths** (playlist order, deduped, non-local and unsupported-extension entries dropped, missing files kept so the tag reader reports them as failed); tags in the file are ignored and re-read. `ImportSession.run(playlists:)` builds one album per track to keep the order.
    - `AlbumBuilder.swift` – groups flat entries into albums by directory + album tag, for imports and loads alike. With `flat: true` every track is an album of its own: a *flat* playlist has no header rows, but positions/playback still work on `(album, track)`.
    - `AlbumKey` – that identity as a string, also the cover cache key.
  - `Artwork/`
    - `AlbumArtProcessor` – per album: pick the source by the `ArtworkSource` priority, extract the image bytes once, shrink, cache.
    - `Thumbnailer` – ImageIO resize to a square `AppConstants.coverThumbnailPixels` JPEG/PNG.
    - `CoverStore` – SQLite cache `.cache/covers.sqlite`, key -> image bytes.
  - `Tags/` – tag reading via AudioToolbox/AVFoundation, album art detection, lyrics (`TrackTags.lyrics`: ID3 `USLT`/`ULT`, Vorbis `LYRICS`/`UNSYNCEDLYRICS`, MP4 `©lyr`, CAF; AudioToolbox doesn't list them, so a file not read through AVFoundation gets an extra pass: FLAC by hand (`FlacComments`), the rest through AVFoundation); see `docs/tag-parsing.md`.
    - `TrailingTags.swift` – recovers values that ID3v1 cut at 30 bytes from a Lyrics3v2/APEv2 block at the end of v1-only MP3s.
  - `Import/`
    - `AudioFileGatherer` – supported extensions, recursive walk, dedupe, natural sort.
    - `ImportSession` – gather -> read tags -> build albums, publishes progress, supports abort (`reload(files:)` = the same for files already in the playlist). As soon as all files of a directory are read, its albums' art is processed concurrently with the remaining reads. Lyrics found in the tags go into the `LyricsStore` as the files are read (`lyricsFound`, the "Lyrics" line of the progress sheet).
  - `Playback/` – see `docs/playback.md` for why it is not `AVPlayer`.
    - `PlaybackEngine` – gapless playback of a current + next track through one `AVAudioPlayerNode`, with fades, exact position, offline rendering for tests.
    - `TrackDecoder` – one file -> stereo float32 chunks at the output rate (own queue, exact length, seeking, resampling).
    - `ChannelDownmix`
    - `PlaybackListener.swift` – `PlaybackListener` (told by `PlaybackState` when a track starts/ends, with the time actually listened to: `PlayProgress`, forward seeks and pauses don't count; also `playlistDidEnd(playlist:)` (default: nothing) when the playlist ran out by itself, not when the user stopped, and `queueDidEnd()` (default: nothing) when "Stop at queue end" stopped playback after the last queued track played out by itself; `LastFMService`, `LyricsService` and `NotificationService` are the listeners, `PlaybackState(listeners:)`), `PlayedTrack`.
    - `PlaybackOrder.swift` – `PlaybackOrder` (`standard`/`trackShuffle`/`albumShuffle`, config `playback.order` = `default`/`track_shuffle`/`album_shuffle`) and `PlaylistEnd` (`stop`/`startOver`, config `playback.at_playlist_end` = `stop`/`start_over`), both set in the Playback menu (submenus "Playback order" / "At playlist end") through `PlaybackState.order`/`atPlaylistEnd`. The order decides what `nextTarget` (queued next track, next-track button/hotkey/media key/Dock menu) and the next-album command do: shuffles are indefinite and never repeat the track/album that just played (the system generator, `PlaybackState.randomIndex` for tests); the pick is made once per current track (`shuffleNext`) so the engine's queue, "next" and re-checks agree. Album shuffle plays the album in order, then the first track of a random other album. With nothing else to pick (one track/album) a shuffle counts as the end of the playlist. `startOver` wraps to the first track (also for the next track/album buttons on the last one). **Previous track/album always go by playlist order** (no history is kept). `randomTrack()`/`randomAlbum()` (the Random hotkeys) use the same picker but always jump at once, whatever the order is. Consecutive open failures are capped (`failures`, 4x track count) so a playlist of missing files can't loop forever with start over/shuffle.
    - `PlaybackQueue.swift` – the play queue (`PlaybackState.queue`; runtime only, nothing is stored). `QueueEntry` = `(playlist name, TrackID)`, a track is in it once, the playing track never is (`PlaybackState.enqueue` leaves it out; an entry leaves the queue, silently, when its track starts: `started`). `isEnabled` ("Enable Queue", config `general.queue_enabled`, default on; off = it is emptied and can't be added to) and `stopAtEnd` ("Stop at queue end", Playback menu, config `playback.stop_at_queue_end`, default off). `onChange` (set by `PlaybackState`) re-hands the engine its next track after every other change. `PlaylistStore` keeps it in step with playlist edits (`remove(ids:)`, `delete`, `rename`).
      **How playback uses it** (`PlaybackState`): `nextTarget` = the first queued track that still exists (`queueTarget`, a `Target` with `queued`; in another playlist than the playing one it moves playback over to it, like a cursor-requested track of the active playlist, `Target.playlist`) before anything else (cursor request, playback order). With the queue played through, playback continues by the order in the playlist of the last queued track, or, with `stopAtEnd`, stops (`queueEnded`: the playing track came from the queue (`playingFromQueue`) and nothing is queued; no "Playlist ended" notification then, but `queueDidEnd` -> "Queue ended" when it ended by itself, not through the next track button). Next track follows it; **everything else that starts something drops the queue** (`dropQueue`): `play(...)` (a track by hand, search results, Return), previous track/album, **next album**, random track/album, `stop()`. Internal stops (`halt()`: a track that is gone, the output failed, the playing playlist deleted) keep it. `clearQueue()` (Clear Queue) empties it and returns to the default mode. While stopped, play/pause/`resume` start the queue if it has tracks (`hasQueuedTracks`, `canPlay`), else the track under the cursor.
    - `ResampleQuality` – `low`/`medium`/`high`/`max` (config `playback.resample_quality`, default `high`); `PlaybackState.resampleQuality` hands it to `PlaybackEngine.resampleQuality`, which gives it to each new `TrackDecoder` (so it applies to tracks opened afterwards).
    - `CodecLabel` – format description such as `MP3 CBR 320k` / `FLAC 24/96` from the opened file, which playback writes back to the playlist together with the real duration.
    - `PlaybackState` (also the commands for search results: `play(trackID:inPlaylist:wholeAlbum:)` and `play(playlist:)`, which open that playlist first; and for hotkeys/media keys: `playPause`/`resume`/`pause` (start the track under the cursor while stopped), `randomTrack`/`randomAlbum` (from the playing playlist, the active one while stopped, never the current one), `volumeUp`/`volumeDown` (5%); `order`/`atPlaylistEnd`) – playlist logic and what the playback block shows, on top of the engine. Also `playingRow` (play symbol) and the Playback menu options `cursorFollowsPlayback`/`playbackFollowsCursor` (config `playback.*`; the UI reports the cursor row via `cursorRow`, which may be in another playlist than the playing one: playback then moves over to it when that track starts).
  - `Hotkeys/` – custom global hotkeys (ported from iCanHazShortcut). Carbon is fine in Core (no UI), AppKit isn't, so modifiers are an own `HotkeyModifiers`.
    - `Hotkey.swift` – a valid key combination (Carbon virtual key code, layout-independent, + modifiers) and its canonical text form (`⌃⌥⇧⌘G`, `F19`; what the config holds and the UI shows). Rules: F-keys/Clear need no modifier, Shift alone only for non-typing keys, everything else needs ⌃/⌥/⌘.
    - `GlobalHotkeyManager.swift` – `HotkeyBackend` (tests use a stub) and its Carbon `RegisterEventHotKey` implementation (no permissions; a taken combination fails with `eventHotKeyExistsErr`).
    - `HotkeyAction.swift` – the ten actions, `search` first; the raw value is the config key (`hotkeys.play_pause`, ...).
    - `HotkeyService.swift` – `HotkeyService.shared`: owns the settings (`mediaKeys`, one `Hotkey` per action), persists them and registers the hotkeys; `start()` at launch. `onSearch` is what the search hotkey does (the window is UI: `AppDelegate` sets it). A combination belongs to one action (setting it moves it), registration failures end up in `failed` (the UI shows them), and nothing is registered while a hotkey is being recorded (`setRecording`, else the combination being recorded would trigger its action).
  - `Migration/LegacyMigration.swift` – migration from the previous (PureBasic) version, which used the same working directory: `isNeeded` (no `config.json`, but a `settings.json`), `loadPlan` (`LegacySettings` from `settings.json` + the file list of `current_state.json`, order and duplicates kept), `LegacySettings.apply(to:screenHeight:)` (Last.fm login, `use_genius` -> LRCLIB, `use_terminal_notifier` -> playback notifications, cursor/playback follows, find/toggle/next/previous shortcuts -> search, play/pause, next/previous track (an empty `find_shortcut` turns the search hotkey off; a migrated hotkey takes the combination from an action that only has it by default), window size/position if within `ConfigLimits`; the old origin is the top left of the main screen; everything else is ignored, with "TODO later" notes in the source) and `cleanUp` (removes everything but `config.json`, `.cache`, `playlists`). Best effort and irreversible.
  - `Constants.swift`, `Log.swift`, `StableHash.swift`
- `UI/` – Views and AppKit glue.
  - `Layout.swift` – layout constants.
  - `MainWindow/`, `Playback/`, `About/` (`AboutView` has the manual "check for updates" link under the version). `PlaybackBlock` shows a lyrics button (`text.page`, right of the codec line) while the playing track has lyrics.
  - `Lyrics/` – `LyricsSheet` (presents the sheet on the main window; one at a time) + `LyricsView`: artist – title, a tag with the source (`embedded`/`lrclib`, + `synced`), then the text. `.playing` follows the player from track to track, `.track(...)` shows one track. Synced lyrics highlight the line being sung and keep it centered while the shown track is the playing one; the position is polled 5x a second from `PlaybackState.livePosition` (`position` only moves in whole seconds), the view is redrawn only when the line changes.
  - `Updates/` – `UpdateSheet` (the sheet offering a release: title, changelog, Skip this version / Later / Update; stays open with a spinner during the installation) + `UpdateActions` (`checkManually(on:)` = the About window's "check for updates" link, with the "latest version"/error alerts; `updateFound` = automatic checks, shown on the main window, dropped with a log line if no window is free; after a successful `install` it quits the app).
  - `Queue/QueueView.swift` – the queue shown in place of the playlist (sidebar item "Queue", first, below it a separator; `PlaylistStore.isQueueShown`, cleared by opening any playlist; the active playlist stays the active one, so File > Add, drops, Import/Export and "Don't group" are disabled meanwhile). It builds a flat `Playlist` with one track per entry (row = place in the queue) and lets `VirtualPlaylistView` show it (`queue:`): rows are `place Artist – Title duration [playlist]` (the playlist label, in `Color.playlistLabel` like the search window's, only with several playlists). Only action: remove (Backspace / context menu), plus Show in Playlist and Reveal in Finder; Return and double click do nothing. The sidebar item's context menu and the Playback menu have Clear Queue.
  - **Keys**: the visible playlist/queue view is the keyboard target of the whole main window, whatever has the key focus (the sidebar after choosing a playlist, ...): one local `NSEvent` monitor in `VirtualPlaylistView` handles ↑/↓ (⇧ extends), Return, Space, Backspace and ⌘R = Reload Tag(s) (`handleKey`; text being edited keeps its keys). There is no `focusable`/`@FocusState` on purpose (it was lost whenever the sidebar was clicked). The monitor outlives the view struct, so it calls the handler the latest `body` stored in `KeyHandlerBox`; never capture view values in it.
  - In a playlist (`VirtualPlaylistView`, `PlaylistItemActions.queueToggle`): **Space** or the context menu's "Enqueue" (shortcut ␣) adds the selection (a track, or all tracks of an album; the playing track is skipped) to the queue; if all of it is queued the same key/item reads "Remove from Queue". Queued tracks show their place (1...99, `Q` beyond) as a capsule in the status column where the play symbol goes (`QueueBadge` in `PlaylistRowView`).
  - `Preferences/` – `PreferencesView`, the Preferences window (⌘,, a `Window` scene): vertical tabs General / Playback / Playlist / Search / Integrations / Hotkeys (Search has the four switches of `SearchSettings`, "Prefer Adding to Queue" shows Off and is disabled while the queue is off; General has the Playback Notifications, Lyrics Support, Enable Queue and Check for Updates switches; Integrations has the Last.fm Connect/Disconnect block and the LRCLIB switch (`LyricsService.isLRCLIBEnabled`); Hotkeys has the media keys switch and one `HotkeyPicker` per action). `LastFMActions` = the UI flows (confirmation sheet `LastFMAuthSheet` + alerts on the Preferences window, `connectionLost` alert + Dock bounce). Controls bind straight to the setting's owner (`PlaybackState.resampleQuality`, `PlaylistStore.tagParsingConcurrency`/`displayAlbumArt`), which persists it via `ConfigStore`.
  - `Hotkeys/` – `HotkeyRecorderField` (the recording control, AppKit) + `HotkeyPicker` (SwiftUI wrapper), and `SystemMediaControls`: the media keys through `MPRemoteCommandCenter` (play/pause/toggle/next/previous) + `MPNowPlayingInfoCenter` (what plays, with artwork) instead of an event tap: no permissions, also serves headsets/AirPods/Control Center. The system sends the keys to the app it considers "now playing", so before something was played they may go to another app (Music). Follows `HotkeyService.mediaKeys`. Handlers/artwork closures are made in `nonisolated` helpers on purpose (the system calls them on its own queue; isolated closures trap).
  - `Search/` – the search window: `SearchPanel` (a non-activating, borderless floating `NSPanel` that takes the key focus like Spotlight/Alfred/Raycast; closes on Esc, a choice or losing key status; doesn't activate the app, so it works the same from ⌘F and the global hotkey; Return/click starts the result through `PlaybackState` and doesn't bring the main window forward, **⌥Return = go to**: opens its playlist with the track/album header selected and scrolled to (`PlaylistStore.reveal`, which the view picks up in `PlayerArea.playlistOpened` via `takeReveal`) and brings the main window forward, **⇧Return = add to queue** (`SearchAction.enqueue`: a track, or all tracks of an album, are added through `PlaybackState.enqueue`; a playlist can't be queued, a track already queued is left alone; the window stays and the result's line shows `#2`/`queued` through `SearchModel.queueMark`; with the queue turned off ⇧Return does nothing and isn't in the legend, `SearchModel.secondaryAction` is `nil`); a legend of these keys is always shown at the bottom (`SearchLegend`, counted in `Layout.searchHeight`); **"Prefer adding to queue" (`SearchSettings.preferAddingToQueue`) swaps Return and ⇧Return** (no effect while the queue is off) (`SearchModel.primaryAction`/`secondaryAction`, which also feed the legend; a click = Return); **with playlists and albums both off the kind tag (`track`) is left out** (`SearchModel.showsKinds`; the playlist tag stays) and the field's placeholder lists what can be found; ignored while an import runs), `SearchModel` (query, results, selection; one search at a time, the newest query wins; Return before the results caught up chooses when they have), `SearchView` (`[track] Artist – Title … [playlist]`, playlist tags only if there are several playlists) and `SearchField` (an `NSTextField` that handles up/down/Return/Esc through its delegate).
  - `Playlist/` – virtualised playlist view: `PlaylistView` (also the empty-playlist placeholder, shown when the loaded active playlist has no tracks), `VirtualPlaylistView`, `PlaylistLayout` (row offsets), row views (a track row shows a `text.page` button right of the title if the track has lyrics, `LyricsService.hasLyrics`, which reads the observable `revision` so rows redraw when LRCLIB finds some; it opens the sheet for that track), `CoverCache` (cached thumbnail from `CoverStore` or generated placeholder). The row context menu/keys (Play, Reveal in Finder, Reload Tag(s) = ⌘R behind the import sheet, Remove from Playlist = Backspace) live in `VirtualPlaylistView` + `PlaylistItemActions`.
  - `Playlist/` reordering (`VirtualPlaylistView` + `PlaylistReorder.swift`): a `DragGesture` (6 pt) on the whole content, not on rows (rows come and go while collapsing). Dragging a **track** moves it inside its album (a track of a flat playlist anywhere); dragging an **album header** shows the playlist **collapsed to its headers** (`ReorderDrag.collapsed`, a `PlaylistLayout(heights:)`; `window`/rows are then album indexes) and on drop expands it again. The dragged item is drawn by `DraggedItemOverlay` at `ReorderDrag.drawnY` (pointer-driven, vertical only; `DragPosition` is observed alone so only the overlay redraws), the other items get `shift(of:)` to make room. `tickDrag` runs on every gesture event **and a 60 Hz timer** (`ReorderBox`, edge autoscroll while the pointer rests). **Scroll position**: on collapse and on expand the item is kept where it is on screen (`viewportOffset(ofContentY:)` before, `scrollContent(_:to:total:using:)` after); the new content's limits are computed by hand because the NSScrollView still has the old document height until SwiftUI's next update (the same call is repeated on the next run-loop turn). The drop applies the move in the store and ends the drag in one go, describing the new content itself (`endDrag(landing:playlist:layout:)`) since this view value still has the old playlist. Esc cancels; no drag in the queue view, during an import or with ⇧/⌘/⌥/⌃ held; clicks are suppressed while/just after a drag (`ReorderBox.suppressClicks`). Only the pressed item moves, not the selection. Pure geometry is tested in `tests/PlaylistReorderTests.swift`; the scroll handling is not covered (AppKit).
  - `Dialogs/` – `Dialogs.swift` + `PlaylistActions.swift`, NSAlert-based flows (`PlaylistActions.exportActive` = the Playlist menu's Export, an `NSSavePanel` with a format popup).
  - The Playback menu (`AppCommands` in `iCanHazMusicApp.swift`) repeats the playback block's buttons (previous album/track, play/pause, next track/album, stop; all go through `PlaybackState`, so they follow the order) and has the "Playback order" / "At playlist end" submenus (inline pickers), the two cursor toggles and, with the queue on, "Stop at queue end" and Clear Queue.
  - `Window/` – `WindowPersistence.swift`, `WindowAccessor.swift`.
  - `Migration/MigrationActions.swift` – the UI of the migration: `askAtLaunch()` runs from `iCanHazMusicApp.init()`, **before any singleton touches the working directory** (Yes / Start from scratch / No = quit); "Yes" puts the old settings into the config right away, `finishAfterLaunch()` (from `AppDelegate`) then reads the old playlist into `main` (`ImportCoordinator.importList`, `ImportSession.run(files:)`; "Don't group" applies to it first), and finally runs `cleanUp`. Note: `NSApp` doesn't exist yet in `askAtLaunch`, use `NSApplication.shared`.
  - `Import/` – `ImportCoordinator` (File menu / drag and drop entry points), Playlist > Import playlist...; **drops: playlist files are imported only if nothing but playlist files was dropped, in a mix with audio files/directories they are ignored (albums often carry their own playlist); File > Add File(s)/Directory never import playlists**, `ImportProgressSheet` + `ImportProgressView` (the modal progress sheet).

## Runtime data
Everything lives in `~/Library/Application Support/iCanHazMusic` (`AppPaths`, see `Constants.swift`). Pass `--workdir /some/dir` (or `--workdir=/some/dir`) to the app to use another directory instead, e.g. a scratch copy:
- `config.json` – read once at startup, written on every setting change (debounced ~250 ms, flushed on quit). Missing/invalid values are reset to defaults and written back.
  - `integrations.lastfm.session` / `username` (empty = not connected; one without the other is reset): the Last.fm session key and account name. Removed on disconnect.
  - `general.playback_notifications` (bool, default true): send system notifications about playback.
  - `general.lyrics_support` (bool, default true): show lyrics (buttons) and look them up; off also turns LRCLIB off in effect.
  - `general.queue_enabled` (bool, default true): the play queue exists (sidebar item, menu items, Space key, markers, adding from search). `playback.stop_at_queue_end` (bool, default false): see `PlaybackQueue.swift`.
  - `general.check_for_updates` (bool, default true): look for a new version at launch and once a day, and offer it. `general.skipped_update` (string, default empty): the version the user chose "Skip this version" for.
  - `integrations.lrclib.enabled` (bool, default false): look up lyrics on LRCLIB for tracks that have none.
  - `playback.volume` (0...1, default 0.7): the volume slider's value, restored on startup.
  - `hotkeys.media_keys` (bool, default true) and `hotkeys.search`/`play_pause`/`next_track`/`previous_track`/`next_album`/`previous_album`/`random_track`/`random_album`/`volume_up`/`volume_down`: hotkey strings (`"⌃⌥P"`), empty = none. Only `search` has a default (`⇧⌘W`, kept if the key is missing, but not if another action has that combination in the file); the others default to none. Invalid or repeated ones are reset to none on load.
  - `search.by_playlist_name` (bool, default true), `search.by_album_name` (bool, default true), `search.fuzzy` (bool, default true), `search.prefer_adding_to_queue` (bool, default false): see `SearchSettings.swift`.
  - `playback.order` (`default`/`track_shuffle`/`album_shuffle`, default `default`) and `playback.at_playlist_end` (`stop`/`start_over`, default `stop`): see `PlaybackOrder.swift`.
  - Preferences: `playback.resample_quality` (`low`/`medium`/`high`/`max`), `playlist.tag_parsing_concurrency` (`0` = auto = number of physical cores, else one of `ConfigLimits.tagParsingConcurrencyOptions`; read when an import starts), `playlist.display_album_art` (off: grouped playlists show no covers and load none, the album headers get shorter; art is cached on import regardless).
- `.cache/covers.sqlite` – album art thumbnails (key = `AlbumKey`, value = JPEG/PNG bytes, 2x the album block cover), rebuilt on import, safe to delete. The thumbnail size is stored in `PRAGMA user_version`; a different size drops the table.
- `.cache/lyrics.sqlite` – lyrics (key = `LyricsKey`, value = source + text + synced LRC text), safe to delete: LRCLIB's come back on the next play, the embedded ones with Reload Tag(s) (nothing reads them from the files at play time).
- `playlists/{name}.json` – one file per playlist; `main.json` is created automatically if there are none.
  - Format: `{"version": 1, "is_flat": false, "next_id": N, "last_played"?: id, "tracks": [{id, path, artist, album, title, trackNumber?, year?, duration?, codec}, ...]}`, a flat array in playlist order (albums are rebuilt on load, album art isn't stored).
  - `id` / `next_id`: see Track IDs above; files from before IDs existed get them on load.
  - `last_played`: ID of the track that was started last; ignored if the playlist has no such track. Opening a playlist selects it and scrolls to it (`PlayerArea.playlistOpened`, `PlaybackState.placeCursor` puts the cursor there without that being a request for `playbackFollowsCursor`).
  - `is_flat` (missing = false) is the Playlist menu's "Don't group by albums": no album blocks, `artist – title` rows, no track numbers.
  - `duration` and `codec` are corrected by playback when the opened file says otherwise.
  - A file that can't be parsed is moved to `{name}.json.broken` and replaced by an empty playlist.

This is the user's real dev data. When testing, prefer seeding/inspecting the files over deleting them, and leave the directory in a sane state afterwards.

## Building and running
- Full dev build + launch: `./build.sh` (clean build, signed with `ICHM_SIGNING_IDENTITY` from `.env` if set (no hardened runtime/timestamp/notarization in dev), else ad-hoc, then **runs the app in the foreground** with `--workdir ~/Library/Application Support/iCanHazMusic-dev` (so the real data in `iCanHazMusic` stays untouched; `dev-release`/`release` builds use the regular directory)). It blocks until the app quits, so give it a `timeout_ms` or don't use it from an agent; the user normally runs it.
- Compile check only: `swift build -c debug --arch arm64`.
- Fast typecheck without SwiftPM: `swiftc -typecheck -parse-as-library -target arm64-apple-macos15.4 $(find src -name '*.swift')`. `src/` is nested, so a plain `src/*.swift` glob doesn't match; list the files explicitly with `find`.
- Tests only: `./build.sh test` (generates the fixtures, then `swift test`; no artifacts). `dev-release` and `release` run the same two steps after the build and abort on a failure; plain `./build.sh` (dev) skips them.
- Scratch experiments go to `tmp/` (git-ignored; never use `dist/`, `build.sh` wipes it); delete them afterwards.

## Tests
`swift test` runs the Swift Testing suite in `tests/` (`@testable import iCanHazMusic`). All suites are nested in `AllTests` and run serialized on the main actor; mark new suites `@MainActor @Suite(...)` inside `extension AllTests`.

- Audio/image fixtures are generated, not committed: run `./tests/prepare-fixtures.sh` once (needs `ffmpeg`, `brew install ffmpeg`; it exits with an error if it is missing). Output goes to `tests/fixtures/generated/` (git-ignored). Tests using them fail with a hint if they weren't generated. Every tagged fixture carries the values in `Fixtures` (`TTitle`/`TArtist`/`TAlbum`/2020/track 3, 2 seconds long); add new fixtures to the script and document them there.
- Tests never touch the real working directory: `ConfigStore(paths:)`, `PlaylistStore(paths:configStore:)`, `CoverStore(url:thumbnailPixels:)`, `LyricsStore(url:)`, `LyricsService(configStore:store:transport:)` and `ImportSession(coverStore:lyricsStore:)` take their locations as parameters, tests use `TempDir` (`tests/Support/Fixtures.swift`). Don't touch `.shared` singletons from tests.
- Playback tests run the engine **offline** (`PlaybackEngine(output: .offline(sampleRate:))`, driven by `EngineRig` in `tests/Support`): the output is compared sample by sample with the generated ramp/tone fixtures (`RampFile`/`ToneFile` in `PlaybackSupport.swift`, files `playback/*` made by `prepare-fixtures.sh`). `PlaybackState(store:engine:tickInterval:)` takes its store and engine as parameters; use `tickInterval: nil` and call `tick()` yourself. Mind the app's default volume of 0.7 when comparing samples.
- Not covered: the real output device (start/stop, device changes; see `docs/playback.md`) and the SwiftUI/AppKit layer.

### Quirks worth knowing
- A **crash** in the test process (a failed `precondition`, a force-unwrapped nil, an ObjC exception) looks like a **hang** of `swift test`: the Swift backtracer symbolicates for minutes. Run with `SWIFT_BACKTRACE=enable=no` to fail fast, and wrap long runs in `timeout`. Test helpers should report problems with `#expect`/return values, not trap.
- SwiftUI does not honor the sidebar's ideal width (it always starts at 140). Don't fight it; the width is saved but not forced.
- SwiftUI re-places the window after the view is attached, so `WindowPersistence` applies the saved frame twice (immediately and on the next run-loop turn) and starts observing changes only after that.
- The saved window size is the whole frame (title bar/toolbar included), not the content size.
- Killing the app with SIGKILL (e.g. a command timeout) can lose the last ≤250 ms of config changes; SIGTERM is fine.

## Rules & Conventions
- Keep config validation limits in `ConfigLimits.swift` (config values outside them are reset to defaults) and layout constants in `Layout.swift`; `Layout` derives shared values from `ConfigLimits`.
- Change settings only through `ConfigStore.shared.update { ... }`; don't write `config.json` elsewhere.
- Playlist name rules and file operations live in `PlaylistStore`; UI flows (prompt, confirm, error) in `PlaylistActions`.
- Use `Log.info` / `Log.error` (stdout, `[iCHM]` prefix) rather than bare `print`.
- Dialogs are `NSAlert` sheets on the main window (`Dialogs`), not SwiftUI alerts.
- Log whatever seems important but not overly noisy.
- There should be no compilation warnings. If you see one, fix it even if it's not from the recent changes.
- Prefer the included tools for reading, writing and editing the files.
